#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "bc694d40-d150-55e6-981f-0ca6b5d91e21" :question/math-academy-id "e-10418" :question/problem "A travel agency offers three itineraries, itinerary $1,$ itinerary $2,$ and $3.$ For each itinerary, customers can choose to travel using their own car or the agency's bus.\n\n- Itinerary $1$ is chosen by $25\\%$ of the customers, and $60\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $2$ is chosen by $30\\%$ of the customers, and $30\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $3$ is chosen by $45\\%$ of the customers, and $20\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\nIf $65\\%$ of all customers travel with children, and $40\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses the bus travels with children? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a customer travels with children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and $I_1, I_2, I_3$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({I}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{I}_{1})=0.6 \\\\  & P({I}_{2})=0.3 &  & P(B|{I}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P({I}_{3})=0.45 &  & P(B|{I}_{3})=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $I_1 \\sqcup I_2  \\sqcup I_3$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(I_1)+ P(I_2) + P(I_3) = 0.25+0.3+0.45 = 1.\n$$\n\n- Since $65\\%$ of customers have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C) = 0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $40\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n\n$$\nP(B | C) = 0.4.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses the bus travels with children, which is represented as $P(C | B).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B|{I}_{1})P({I}_{1})+P(B|{I}_{2})P({I}_{2})+P(B|{I}_{3})P({I}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.65)}{(0.6)(0.25)+(0.3)(0.3)+(0.2)(0.45)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.26}{0.33} \\\\  & ≈0.788,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166697 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "699d9c0c-bb98-5710-8d98-10b6f35ab202" :question/math-academy-id "e-8911" :question/problem "The disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup A_3$ of events $A_1,A_2,A_3$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P(A_1|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.23, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.26, & P({A}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.18, & P({A}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup \\ldots \\sqcup A_n$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{P(B)} = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,\\ldots,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup A_3$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP(A_1) + P(A_2) + P(A_3) = 1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})) \\\\  & =1-(0.3+0.4) \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.23)(0.3)}{(0.23)(0.3)+(0.26)(0.3)+(0.18)(0.4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.069}{0.219} \\\\  & ≈0.315.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166698 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "284f5029-19eb-53d4-a3b7-0f66d33b9cc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8912" :question/problem "Potatoes produced by a particular farm come in three different varieties, variety $1,$ variety $2,$ and variety $3.$\n\n- $36\\%$ of the potatoes are of variety $1,$ and $20\\%$ of those potatoes weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g.$\n\n- $17\\%$ of the potatoes are of variety $2,$ and $6\\%$ of those potatoes weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g.$\n\n- $47\\%$ of the potatoes are of the variety $3.$\n\nIf $16\\%$ of potatoes from the entire farm are sold to a local restaurant, $40\\%$ of potatoes sold to the restaurant weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ and $15\\%$ of potatoes that weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$ are sold to the restaurant, what is the probability that a randomly selected potato from variety $3$ weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that a potato is sold to the restaurant, $W$ be the event that a potato weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ and $V_1, V_2, V_3$ be the event that a potato is of variety $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({V}_{1})=0.36 &  & P(W|{V}_{1})=0.2 \\\\  & P({V}_{2})=0.17 &  & P(W|{V}_{2})=0.06 \\\\  & P({V}_{3})=0.47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $V_1 \\sqcup V_2  \\sqcup V_3$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(V_1)+ P(V_2) + P(V_3) = 0.36+0.17+0.47 = 1.\n$$\n\n- Since $16\\%$ of potatoes from the entire farm are sold to a local restaurant, we have\n\n$$\nP(R) = 0.16.\n$$\n\n- Since $40\\%$ potatoes sold to the restaurant weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W | R) = 0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $15\\%$ of potatoes that weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$ are sold to the restaurant, we have\n\n$$\nP(R | W) = 0.15.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen potato of variety $3$ weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ which is represented as $P(W | V_3).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|W) & =\\frac{P(W|R)P(R)}{P(W)} \\\\ P(R|W) & =\\frac{P(W|R)P(R)}{P(W|{V}_{1})P({V}_{1})+P(W|{V}_{2})P({V}_{2})+P(W|{V}_{3})P({V}_{3})} \\\\ 0.15 & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.16)}{(0.2)(0.36)+(0.06)(0.17)+P(W|{V}_{3})(0.47)} \\\\ 0.15 & =\\frac{0.064}{0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(W | V_3),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.15(0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3})) & =0.064 \\\\ 0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.064}{0.15} \\\\ 0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{32}{75} \\\\ 0.47P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{32}{75}-0.0822 \\\\ P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.47}(\\frac{32}{75}-0.0822) \\\\ P(W|{V}_{3}) & ≈0.733\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82c6c187-790b-565e-aa8f-aebe0211164d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163775} {:answer/id #uuid "8695e504-405f-5075-af32-0f515766063d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163776} {:answer/id #uuid "e432c098-2322-51e8-bf1e-f92e1f9dea68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163777} {:answer/id #uuid "e975a2a8-ee28-5a19-85c9-a464db15b432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163778} {:answer/id #uuid "68b97b4b-f74b-5cbb-b512-1d895d9baa07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163779}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3fdaa067-53d0-53b1-9c47-f58c53089439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163774}] :question/id #uuid "20ddccd9-19cf-5781-a2f6-c8e6f37cdb70" :question/math-academy-id "q-147132" :question/problem "The disjoint union $A_{1}⊔A_{2}⊔A_{3}$ of events $A_{1}, A_{2}, A_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P(A_{3} \\mid B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$P(B\\begin{vmatrix}A_{1}) = 0.5, \\\\ P(B \\mid A_{2}) = 0.6,\\end{vmatrix}P(A_{2}) = 0.2; P(B \\mid A_{3}) = 0.1, \\mid P(A_{3}) = 0.7$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5c4998-4883-5ba4-a08f-5c9044a9ad93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.584" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163781} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eec898-008f-5aeb-9d82-da5f4e5795a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.642" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163782} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f06ee9-62a4-5d90-a2a6-5b8a41e3e676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.527" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163783} {:answer/id #uuid "088444df-24a0-506a-90eb-e82abac77d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.621" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163784} {:answer/id #uuid "4988a61c-c2b9-57dd-87f0-60ed10f9fa86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.592" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4988a61c-c2b9-57dd-87f0-60ed10f9fa86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.592" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163785} :answer-field/id #uuid "4141da06-c478-52fb-832c-70aa2c84d531" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e39e7e2c-b39e-5302-b92e-8f13f6ffd98c" :question/math-academy-id "q-147235" :question/problem "The students in a particular school are split among three classes, class $1,$ class $2,$ and class $3.$\n\n- Class $1$ contains $25%$ of the students in the school, and $55%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $2$ contains $35%$ of the students in the school, and $40%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $3$ contains $40%$ of the students in the school.\n\nIf $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, $80%$ of female students take biology, and $70%$ of biology students are female, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from class $3$ takes biology? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $B$ be the event that a student takes biology, and ${C}_{1},{C}_{2},{C}_{3}$ be the event that a student is in class $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({C}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{C}_{1})=0.55 \\\\  & P({C}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(B|{C}_{2})=0.4 \\\\  & P({C}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})+P({C}_{2})+P({C}_{3})=0.25+0.35+0.4=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females take biology, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of biology students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|B)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student from class $3$ takes biology, which is represented as $P(B|{C}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.45)}{(0.55)(0.25)+(0.4)(0.35)+P(B|{C}_{3})(0.4)} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{0.36}{0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{C}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.7(0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})) & =0.36 \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.36}{0.7} \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35} \\\\ 0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775 \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.4}(\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775) \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & ≈0.592\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adaca2a-25b8-5492-8e73-6d48d5c107c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486615} {:answer/id #uuid "5becaaa9-5f26-54d9-87bd-69282364be70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486800} {:answer/id #uuid "75086fb7-7e32-5fdd-b1ef-015370496b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486848} {:answer/id #uuid "7872a91f-ffad-5a9d-bb60-967650a7e581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486858} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ab6e75-9266-51d3-85df-bd426a296750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75086fb7-7e32-5fdd-b1ef-015370496b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486848} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b922273-f540-5f67-8f2c-45b151c3e563" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43f08f68-330a-59ee-956f-33e7cbacb451" :question/math-academy-id "q-147882" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{5}, & P({A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=\\frac{1}{8}, & P({A}_{2})=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=1, & P({A}_{3})=\\frac{7}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{7}{20}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{8})(\\frac{2}{5})}{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{4})+(\\frac{1}{8})(\\frac{2}{5})+(1)(\\frac{7}{20})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{20})}{(\\frac{9}{20})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a9f222-5e6c-584f-8a20-252c749fb9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486752} {:answer/id #uuid "5a778b05-75eb-5814-bf54-a782e3521987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486795} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6aee04-ab75-5dd5-ac10-45761ebcebf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486826} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2037c8-40f3-5d56-9c3b-51a6e2eb5517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487043} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd5001a-0138-50b7-bf54-a87a859ada26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b6aee04-ab75-5dd5-ac10-45761ebcebf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486826} :answer-field/id #uuid "f77d5683-3c91-57b4-a6c6-baacce64e39e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c768c6df-9213-55c1-bcb4-06af864aa6e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-147887" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3},{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{1}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{5}, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0, & P({A}_{2})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{5}, & P({A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{4})=\\frac{2}{5}, & P({A}_{4})=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=4,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})) \\\\  & =1-(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})+P(B|{A}_{4})P({A}_{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{3}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{3}{8})+(0)(\\frac{1}{4})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{8})+(\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{40})}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b28de4-aa80-5f7f-a8f5-b4f77a28ca63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486638} {:answer/id #uuid "6dca3446-f03b-53af-ba74-95ebd9f19f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486836} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d76d72-edbb-5e01-b342-efdf9523a5a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487127} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ffd134-c98f-5abb-a2f0-f7d9bf8bae1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487128} {:answer/id #uuid "faeb72a2-110a-55d3-ba57-0a887a322d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13b28de4-aa80-5f7f-a8f5-b4f77a28ca63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486638} :answer-field/id #uuid "00cf1d61-f08e-59ba-9b21-977d6711da5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "521e5a18-fa22-58cf-9f82-7b4aa14a1323" :question/math-academy-id "q-148152" :question/problem "Some researchers want to test the belief that teenagers who play an instrument are more likely to be good at math. They collect data on a large sample of teenagers that play an instrument.\n\n- $30%$ of teenagers play piano, and $50%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $20%$ of teenagers play violin, and $60%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $50%$ of teenagers play other instruments, and $47%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\nIf $10%$ of teenagers started to play music before $6$ years old, and $58%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, what is the probability that a randomly chosen teenager that is good at math started to play music before $6$ years old? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.5 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{2})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $58%$ of students who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.58.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who is good at math started to play before $6$ years old, which is represented as $P(B|M).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.58)(0.1)}{(0.5)(0.3)+(0.6)(0.2)+(0.47)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.058}{0.505} \\\\  & ≈0.115,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08940ee3-c31c-52b4-8370-33f3d3aa8657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.475" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486610} {:answer/id #uuid "1faf867c-bba7-5f95-927b-c813a9e9dcf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.623" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486656} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa3b441-f3be-5a7b-8222-c944862c87a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486692} {:answer/id #uuid "6eabcb60-c57c-5edd-bee7-61fe19a4610b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486838} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1bcac5-e08b-5cb7-8e74-64d1c2bee8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.740" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff1bcac5-e08b-5cb7-8e74-64d1c2bee8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.740" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487137} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a4501a8-443f-5ae0-aa78-dccc204637f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9b22d29b-922e-508b-b541-5acf3087f769" :question/math-academy-id "q-148523" :question/problem "A company is selling a new product on three e-commerce platforms, platform $1,$ platform $2,$ and platform $3.$\n\n- $44%$ of customers bought the product on platform $1,$ and $70%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $25%$ of customers bought the product on platform $2,$ and $85%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $31%$ of customers bought the product on platform $3.$\n\nIf $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied with the product, and $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.7 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.44+0.25+0.31=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.6.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied with the product, which is represented as $P(S|{P}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S|{P}_{1})P({P}_{1})+P(S|{P}_{2})P({P}_{2})+P(S|{P}_{3})P({P}_{3})} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{(0.60)(0.25)}{(0.7)(0.44)+(0.85)(0.25)+P(S|{P}_{3})(0.31)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{P}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2(0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.2} \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205 \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.31}(\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205) \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & ≈0.740\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88acdedb-86f7-5376-9663-ed92e647a982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.174" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163787} {:answer/id #uuid "b8298e93-12cb-5948-9b0c-4d75b7d3a684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.199" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163788} {:answer/id #uuid "cca46cae-3f7b-5c75-a82f-2a44e0f21afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.203" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163789} {:answer/id #uuid "1bae2866-554f-511e-86e4-7f2ca81b63aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163790} {:answer/id #uuid "7352704f-f734-5a12-b655-8b2ef85bf0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.191" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8298e93-12cb-5948-9b0c-4d75b7d3a684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.199" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163788} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a0e7e2-d14d-5d36-a9fb-b4d391b04b02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dea495bf-07d9-54cd-baea-bb953539d3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-148673" :question/problem "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1,$ mode $2,$ and mode $3.$ They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $40%$ of their muffins, and $85%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $88%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $20%$ of all muffins was prepared with yeast A, and $87%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, what is the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is done with yeast A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a muffin is made with yeast A, $G$ be the event that a muffin turns out well, and ${M}_{1},{M}_{2},{M}_{3}$ be the event that a muffin is cooked with mode $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({M}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(G|{M}_{1})=0.9 \\\\  & P({M}_{2})=0.4 &  & P(G|{M}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({M}_{3})=0.3 &  & P(G|{M}_{3})=0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})+P({M}_{2})+P({M}_{3})=0.3+0.4+0.3=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of muffins are made with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since $87%$ of muffins made with yeast A turn out well, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.87.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is made with yeast A, which is represented as $P(A|G).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(G|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(G|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.87)(0.2)}{(0.9)(0.3)+(0.85)(0.4)+(0.88)(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.174}{0.874} \\\\  & ≈0.199,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3ef3be7-aacb-5053-aed6-a050c2e08656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163793} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca4ad11-a9a2-5e9d-b942-28716e357410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.562" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163794} {:answer/id #uuid "2451e569-ab1f-53fd-b6b7-a4e2c0b10203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.682" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163795} {:answer/id #uuid "4280b356-4cc6-5e95-8643-cd0889d3d58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.717" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163796} {:answer/id #uuid "7c305fd3-da6a-5509-82b5-27016b5d41be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.697" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163797}] :answer-field/id #uuid "be8135a5-1fa3-5e9d-9907-a7017aa5aba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163792}] :question/id #uuid "5b92de6f-2dd5-5916-8078-94243b8290b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-148674" :question/problem "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1$, mode $2$, and mode $3$. They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $45%$ of their muffins, and $80%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $35%$ of their muffins.\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $20%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $65%$ of all muffins were prepared with yeast A, $82%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, and $68%$ of good muffins were prepared with yeast A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen muffin cooked with mode $2$ turned out well? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aad9a2ec-8c38-50c5-a148-e148cf1a201f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.307" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493659} {:answer/id #uuid "cb08ac4c-bcdb-5bf3-803d-48abd3c44b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.546" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493717} {:answer/id #uuid "db3514aa-7da2-58cd-a765-4883b4c73d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493736} {:answer/id #uuid "e44bfd71-59f5-5456-8e29-b331fc4b994f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.398" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493751} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ca70ca-440e-5d09-bd91-1a12c84d2bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.252" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e44bfd71-59f5-5456-8e29-b331fc4b994f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.398" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493751} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2b5eda7-fc54-5354-9c43-01751b2546c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "deb0627a-b1f4-5ecd-95e2-372ffa642f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-149493" :question/problem "A volleyball school for children splits the players into three teams, team $1$, team $2,$ and team $3.$ \n\n- $44%$ of children are in team $1,$ and $23%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $32%$ of children are in team $2,$ and $26%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $24%$ of children are in team $3.$\n\nIf $7%$ of all children can play in the blocker position, $40%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, and $10%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child in team $3$ has been playing for over two years? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in the blocker position, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over two years, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.23 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.26 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.44+0.32+0.24=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $7%$ of children can play in the blocker position, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.07.\n$$\n\n- Since $40%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|I)=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child in team 3 has been playing for over two years, which is represented as $P(I|{T}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\ P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.07)}{(0.23)(0.44)+(0.26)(0.32)+P(I|{T}_{3})(0.24)} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(I|{T}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1(0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})) & =0.028 \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1} \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25} \\\\ 0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844 \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.24}(\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844) \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & ≈0.398\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05268a54-1ca3-5236-8a32-72d18477c132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.463" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486603} {:answer/id #uuid "2663f13b-e38f-5407-950a-5fe65e600172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.354" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486669} {:answer/id #uuid "3a52d0ba-3076-551b-ab78-67b5cf870df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486719} {:answer/id #uuid "a5af02ee-9d40-5c6e-b134-2b707a5335a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.323" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486971} {:answer/id #uuid "afb3e40b-8ac2-504a-9793-b673024033ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.904" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2663f13b-e38f-5407-950a-5fe65e600172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.354" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486669} :answer-field/id #uuid "698fc6a4-867b-5609-8d53-bcc56debe7fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa54d1c3-840f-55dc-9c9a-7cb1c9435baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-159358" :question/problem "A travel agency offers three itineraries, itinerary $1,$ itinerary $2,$ and itinerary $3.$ For each itinerary, customers can decide to travel using their own car or the agency's bus.\n\n- Itinerary $1$ is chosen by $60%$ of the customers, and $10%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $2$ is chosen by $20%$ of the customers, and $25%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $3$ is chosen by $20%$ of the customers.\n\nIf $30%$ of all customers travel with children, $50%$ of customers with children choose the bus, and $83%$ of customers that choose the bus travel with children, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ takes the bus? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a customer has children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and ${I}_{1},{I}_{2},{I}_{3}$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({I}_{1})=0.6 &  & P(B|{I}_{1})=0.1 \\\\  & P({I}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(B|{I}_{2})=0.25 \\\\  & P({I}_{3})=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${I}_{1}⊔{I}_{2}⊔{I}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({I}_{1})+P({I}_{2})+P({I}_{3})=0.6+0.2+0.2=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of customers have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $50%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|C)=0.5.\n$$\n\n- Since $83%$ of customers that choose the bus have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|B)=0.83.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ travels by bus, which is represented as $P(B|{I}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B|{I}_{1})P({I}_{1})+P(B|{I}_{2})P({I}_{2})+P(B|{I}_{3})P({I}_{3})} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.3)}{(0.1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.2)+P(B|{I}_{3})(0.2)} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{I}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.83(0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.83} \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83} \\\\ 0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83}-0.11 \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{15}{83}-0.11) \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & ≈0.354\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}]}
